<UserControl x:Class="UI.AmbientBackground"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             mc:Ignorable="d" IsHitTestVisible="False">
    <!--
        Ambient background: a looping, muted video clip (Assets/ambient_loop.mp4 -
        a trimmed, slowed-down, seamlessly-crossfaded segment derived from the user's
        source footage) stretched to fill the window, with a translucent dark scrim on
        top so foreground text/tiles stay legible.

        THE BLUR IS BAKED INTO THE VIDEO FILE, not applied at runtime. It used to be a
        WPF BlurEffect (radius 28) on BOTH players — a full-frame per-pixel shader,
        running twice, every frame, forever, on a machine whose whole job is to leave
        headroom for games. Pre-blurring with ffmpeg (gblur sigma=9) gives an identical
        look for zero GPU cost, and as a bonus the blurred footage compresses far
        better: the asset went from 2.4 MB to 584 KB. The unblurred source is kept as
        ambient_loop_original.mp4 in case the look ever needs re-tuning.

        Two overlapping MediaElements (PlayerA/PlayerB) crossfade into each other
        near the loop point instead of one player hard-restarting (Position=0 + Play())
        on MediaEnded, which causes a visible decoder seek/re-buffer stutter even
        though the underlying video file's own content already loops seamlessly.
    -->
    <Grid Background="#06070B" ClipToBounds="True">
        <MediaElement x:Name="PlayerA"
                      LoadedBehavior="Manual"
                      UnloadedBehavior="Manual"
                      Stretch="UniformToFill"
                      IsMuted="True"
                      Loaded="PlayerA_Loaded"/>

        <MediaElement x:Name="PlayerB"
                      LoadedBehavior="Manual"
                      UnloadedBehavior="Manual"
                      Stretch="UniformToFill"
                      IsMuted="True"
                      Opacity="0"/>

        <!-- Frosted tint: a soft translucent white wash on top of the blur reads as
             "frosted glass" rather than just a blurred video, plus the existing dark
             scrim underneath it for text legibility. -->
        <Rectangle Fill="#8A06070B"/>
        <Rectangle Fill="#14FFFFFF"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
